Subject: ancestral range estimation using higher taxa
Message-ID: <p8ElJCXMIPE.gyqkeHEBLZcJ@biogeobears.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/biogeobears/c/p8ElJCXMIPE/m/gyqkeHEBLZcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Nick and other biogeobearers,

This is a more general question regarding the use of BioGeoBears (or other model-based methods) when the tips of the tree are not species but higher taxa. I don’t remember a discussion of this issue in the literature.

My tree includes a single representative for a number of families.
Typically, these families are distributed across many, sometimes all, areas. In principle, the occupation of multiple areas by the clade can be modeled by multiple dispersal events along the long terminal branch. However, per-lineage dispersal rates will be overestimated since the resultant distribution could be the product of dispersal events in multiple lineages, not the single lineage suggested by the terminal branch. Because terminal branches do not contain speciation events, there is no vicariance and areas get accumulated. Basically, it seems that using higher taxa can overestimate the dispersal rate.
Does anyone have any insight son this?

A practical difficulty is that the number of areas for ancestors cannot be restricted to fewer than the number of areas in terminal clades, and therefore potentially resulting in ancestors occupying too many areas, compared to extant species, which in my dataset rarely occupy mor than one or two.

Any comments will be very helpful.

Cheers,

Santiago

