From u115d05260b0c341a at groups.google.com  Tue Nov 11 23:01:06 2014
From: u115d05260b0c341a at groups.google.com (Nick Matzke)
Date: Tue, 11 Nov 2014 23:01:06 +0000
Subject: Re: ancestral range estimation using higher taxa
Message-ID: <p8ElJCXMIPE.2VDxeqhUvx4J@biogeobears.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <p8ElJCXMIPE.gyqkeHEBLZcJ@biogeobears.groups.google.com>
References: <p8ElJCXMIPE.gyqkeHEBLZcJ@biogeobears.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/biogeobears/c/p8ElJCXMIPE/m/2VDxeqhUvx4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Santiago!

My thought, briefly, is that because BioGeoBEARS, Lagrange, etc. are using a probabilistic models, and these models assume processes that operate on lineages or species (on branches: range expansion, range contraction; at speciation: vicariance, subset sympatry, narrow sympatry, jump dispersal in +J models, etc.), it is not particularly meaningful to put families or other higher taxa at the tips. These tip ranges are then the ranges of a bunch of species combined, and it's not clear how the species-level processes "should" explain these ranges in the model.

Some alternatives:

1. Make each family tip the age of the common ancestor of the family, and put in the best-guess (majority rule, probabilistic sample of the tip ranges, etc.) range as the range of that common ancestor species

2. If you don't have a within-family phylogeny, make random subtrees of the species and place them on the time-scaled tips, then repeat e.g. 100 times...

Cheers!
Nick

On Monday, November 10, 2014 4:33:26 AM UTC+1, Santiago Claramunt wrote:
> Dear Nick and other biogeobearers,
>
> This is a more general question regarding the use of BioGeoBears (or other model-based methods) when the tips of the tree are not species but higher taxa. I don’t remember a discussion of this issue in the literature.
>
> My tree includes a single representative for a number of families.
> Typically, these families are distributed across many, sometimes all, areas. In principle, the occupation of multiple areas by the clade can be modeled by multiple dispersal events along the long terminal branch. However, per-lineage dispersal rates will be overestimated since the resultant distribution could be the product of dispersal events in multiple lineages, not the single lineage suggested by the terminal branch. Because terminal branches do not contain speciation events, there is no vicariance and areas get accumulated. Basically, it seems that using higher taxa can overestimate the dispersal rate.
> Does anyone have any insight son this?
>
> A practical difficulty is that the number of areas for ancestors cannot be restricted to fewer than the number of areas in terminal clades, and therefore potentially resulting in ancestors occupying too many areas, compared to extant species, which in my dataset rarely occupy mor than one or two.
>
> Any comments will be very helpful.
>
> Cheers,
>
> Santiago
>
>
>
>
>

