Hi Nick,
I agree with you; probabilistic models are based on lineages/species; it doesn’t make much sense to use them with higher taxa. I just wanted to include a probabilistic method in addition to non-parametric/parsimony. In all cases, I take the results with a grain of salt. If the different methods agree, there is some signal there.

An additional question. As usual when there are many areas involved, the plots are not easy to follow. I wonder if an alternative summary of the data may be useful here. I’m thinking that in the face of high uncertainty I may not be interested in comparing the marginal probabilities of each combination of areas. Instead I would like to see the likelihood of the ancestor being present in each of the basic areas, i.e. instead of depicting the likelihoods of A, AB, AC, BC, and ABC, just show the probabilities of A, B and C, where the relative likelihood of A  would be the sum of the likelihood of all the combinations in which A is involved (A, AB, AC, ABC).

Dies this make sense?

Thanks!

Santiago

